_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 35
---
order: 35
---
short_label: I.35
---
statement: Parallelograms which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.
---
proof:

Let *ABCD* and *EBCF* be parallelograms on the same base *BC* and in the same parallels *AF* and *BC.*

I say that *ABCD* equals the parallelogram *EBCF.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="300" height="210" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.35 diagram" src="propI35.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.35",
        elements: [
            "B;point;free;70,180",
            "C;point;free;160,180",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "A;point;free;30,40",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "D;point;parallelogram;A,B,C",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "E1;point;free;10000,0",
            "E';point;extend;A,D,A,E1;0;0",
            "E;point;lineSegmentSlider;D,E',180,40",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "F;point;parallelogram;E,B,C",
            "CF;line;connect;C,F",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F",
            "G;point;intersection;CD,BE"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since *ABCD* is a parallelogram, therefore *AD* equals *BC.* [!just I.34]

For the same reason *EF* equals *BC,* so that *AD* also equals *EF.* And *DE* is common, therefore the whole *AE* equals the whole *DF.* [!just C.N.1; C.N.2]

But *AB* also equals *DC.* Therefore the two sides *EA* and *AB* equal the two sides *FD* and *DC* respectively, and the angle *FDC* equals the angle *EAB,* the exterior equals the interior. Therefore the base *EB* equals the base *FC,* and the triangle *EAB* equals the triangle *FDC.* [!just I.34; I.29; I.4]

Subtract *DGE* from each. Then the trapezium *ABGD* which remains equals the trapezium *EGCF* which remains. [!just C.N.3]

Add the triangle *GBC* to each. Then the whole parallelogram *ABCD* equals the whole parallelogram *EBCF.* [!just C.N.2]

Therefore *parallelograms which are on the same base and in the same parallels equal one another.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

Euclid's proof specifically treats the case when the point *D* lies between *A* and *E* in which case subtraction of a triangle is necessary. There are other cases to consider, for instance, when *E* lies between *A* and *D.* In that case the point *G* is irrelevant and the trapezium *BCED* may be added to the congruent triangles *ABE* and *DCF* to derive the conclusion. Euclid often supplies a proof for only one case, although occasionally he gives proofs for two or three cases.

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="270" height="170" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.35 alternate case" src="propI35a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "I.35",
        elements: [
            "B;point;free;60,140",
            "C;point;free;170,140",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "A;point;free;30,40",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "D;point;parallelogram;A,B,C",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "E;point;lineSegmentSlider;A,D,120,40",
            "BE;line;connect;B,E",
            "F;point;parallelogram;E,B,C",
            "CF;line;connect;C,F",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

#### Rectilinear figures as magnitudes

We see how Euclid treats figures as magnitudes by adding as subtracting them. The triangles *EAB* and *FDC* are shown directly to be equal. Then the triangle *DGE,* which is contained in each, is subtracted from each, and Euclid concludes that the remaining trapezia *ABGD* and *EGCF* are therefore equal. These trapezia are not congruent, but they do have the same area. Next, the triangle *GBC* is added to each trapezium to conclude the two parallelograms *ABCD* and *EBCF* are equal.

These are the same kinds of cut-and-paste operations that Euclid used on lines and angles earlier in Book I, but these are applied to rectilinear figures. In later books cut-and-paste operations will be applied to other kinds of magnitudes such as solid figures and arcs of circles.

#### Use of Proposition 35

This proposition is used in the next two propositions and in @XI.31.
